</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nuScenes Retrieval Dataset Results. </span>
As shown in <a href="#S4.F6" title="In 4.2 Main Experiment Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Progressive Gaussian Transformer with Anisotropy-aware Sampling for Open Vocabulary Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, our method achieves a visible mean Average Precision (mAP(v)) of <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">21.2</span> on the nuScenes retrieval dataset. This outperforms the existing vision-based method, LangOcc, which achieves 18.2. This improvement demonstrates the effectiveness of progressive Gaussian modeling in enhancing both accuracy and robustness for 3D open-vocabulary retrieval tasks. Note that GaussTR does not report results on the nuScenes retrieval dataset and cannot be evaluated using LiDAR points. Qualitative results in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Progressive Gaussian Transformer with Anisotropy-aware Sampling for Open Vocabulary Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> further illustrate our system’s language-based retrieval capabilities. Queries such as ”Locate the cars” and ”Locate the garbage bin” are precisely grounded within the predicted 3D occupancy grid.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
