<figure id="S4.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:139.1pt;"><img src="2510.04759v1/nuscenes_retrieval_dataset_result.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:183/101;" width="183" height="101" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>SOTA comparison on the <span id="S4.F6.fig2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nuScenes retrieval</span> dataset.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Depth Estimation Results. </span> 
We evaluate the geometric accuracy of our Gaussian scene representation via quantitative depth estimation, presented in <a href="#S4.F6" title="In 4.2 Main Experiment Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Progressive Gaussian Transformer with Anisotropy-aware Sampling for Open Vocabulary Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, accompanied by depth visualizations in <a href="#A3.F9" title="In C.2 BEV Visualization ‣ Appendix C Additional Visualization Results ‣ Progressive Gaussian Transformer with Anisotropy-aware Sampling for Open Vocabulary Occupancy Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. Remarkably, although supervision is derived from depth maps of Metric3D V2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, our model produces depth estimates that exceed the original labels, achieving an error of 0.139, corresponding to an +18.2% boost. This improvement results from geometric constraints imposed by multi-view depth consistency and feature coherence, which help maintain smooth and accurate scene geometry even in challenging regions.</p>
</div>
